When I "finished" 3.1.1, I was pleased to see cavemen, priests, and so
forth as opponents on the Astral plane.  There should be demigods
there, ready to fight!

Then I fought one.  Whap, whap, whap, whap, dead.  Very disappointing.
He didn't do anything interesting at all!  I looked at the pile he
left behind, and the most exciting thing in there was a food ration.

Demigods should be scary!  If there are any artifacts left in the
game, give them to the demigods!  After all, they needed to pick up
the things to ascend themselves, right?  Why shouldn't they still have
them?

Start with the really nasty artifacts:  Excalibur, Stormbringer,
Vorpal Blade, the Tsurugi of Muramasa, Magicbane and Grayswandir.
Make sure they're all enchanted to +4 or +5.  After that, go with
the artifacts people can be resistant to:  Frost/Fire Brand and
Mjollnir.  Then go with Snickersnee and Cleaver.
Finally, if there are any weaponless demigods left, go with
the artifacts for "other" monsters, like Ogresmasher, Giantslayer, and
so on.

Would getting hit with Mjollnir nuke your wands and rings?  If so, put
it in the first "nasty" category.

Actually, it might be better to assign artifacts by class.  I.e., give
Magicbane to a wizard demigod, Mjollnir to a valkyrie, Excalibur to
a knight, and so forth.

Better yet, if a quest artifact is a weapon, give that to the demigod.
I think this works for the Sceptre of Might (caveman), Tsurugi of
Muramasa (samurai) and Staff of Aesculapius (healer).

Give the demigods a full complement of armor.  This means dragon scale
mail, boots of speed, cloaks of protection and displacement, helms,
shields possibly of reflection, rings of protection, and gauntlets
(possibly of power).

Of course, they should _all_ be wearing amulets of lifesaving.

This would not be unbalancing.  By this point in the game, characters
should have everything they need.  Giving them the extra equipment
won't help, but will just add weight.  The nasty effects of the Vorpal
Blade and the Tsuguri could be avoided by using an amulet of
lifesaving or two (one guaranteed in Vlad's tower, remember?).

To make killing the demigods worthwhile, they should each carry six
or seven gems, all valuable (no glass).  Gems are the only thing in the
game an ascending character _really_ wants.

Hmmm.  I just had a look at the source.  The other character monsters
at the end are not demigods.  They are characters who failed because
they all had "fake" Amulets!  It looks like my gem suggestion was
already done.  However, there is no chance of one of these getting an
artifact.  Also, there is only a _possibility_ of getting any piece of
armor (possibly dragon scale mail).  This should be almost guaranteed.

